WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:05.017
Lunar Site Planner answers one question for the Moon's south pole:

2
00:00:05.017 --> 00:00:06.555
at this place and time,

3
00:00:06.555 --> 00:00:08.834
will a lander get sunlight for power,

4
00:00:08.834 --> 00:00:10.796
and Earth in view to call home?

5
00:00:10.796 --> 00:00:13.659
Here's Professional mode, one feature at a time.

6
00:00:14.309 --> 00:00:16.380
On the left is the mission flow:

7
00:00:16.380 --> 00:00:18.669
five steps, from a site to a report.

8
00:00:18.669 --> 00:00:20.521
The Moon sits in the middle.

9
00:00:20.521 --> 00:00:23.192
The pill on top says what's true right now,

10
00:00:23.192 --> 00:00:26.298
and the timeline below covers the next thirty days.

11
00:00:26.948 --> 00:00:28.167
Step one, Site.

12
00:00:28.167 --> 00:00:31.532
Search by name, filter by mission, type coordinates,

13
00:00:31.532 --> 00:00:34.839
or take one of the best spots for the coming month.

14
00:00:34.839 --> 00:00:38.959
Every listed site and Artemis Three region is marked on the Moon.

15
00:00:39.609 --> 00:00:42.294
The gold line points from your site to the Sun,

16
00:00:42.294 --> 00:00:43.965
and the blue line to Earth.

17
00:00:43.965 --> 00:00:45.485
Right now the Sun is up,

18
00:00:45.485 --> 00:00:47.612
but Earth is just below the skyline,

19
00:00:47.612 --> 00:00:49.081
so the pill says NO GO.

20
00:00:49.731 --> 00:00:52.986
Open Map layers to colour the Moon by sunlight,

21
00:00:52.986 --> 00:00:55.320
Earth in view, or both together:

22
00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:57.715
right now, over your thirty days,

23
00:00:57.715 --> 00:00:59.435
or across all of 2027.

24
00:00:59.435 --> 00:01:02.936
Never-sunlit ground, height and slope are here too.

25
00:01:03.586 --> 00:01:07.364
The South-Pole Map shows the same region from straight above,

26
00:01:07.364 --> 00:01:11.650
with average sunlight or Earth visibility laid over real NASA imagery.

27
00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:13.409
Step two, Date.

28
00:01:13.409 --> 00:01:16.102
The calendar colours every day for this site:

29
00:01:16.102 --> 00:01:18.425
green for sunlight and Earth together,

30
00:01:18.425 --> 00:01:20.960
amber for power only, red for no sunlight.

31
00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:23.020
Or press Find the next good date,

32
00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:25.079
and it searches the year for you.

33
00:01:25.729 --> 00:01:28.291
Press Play, or drag along the timeline,

34
00:01:28.291 --> 00:01:30.682
to watch the Sun circle the horizon,

35
00:01:30.682 --> 00:01:33.814
and see exactly when power and radio come and go.

36
00:01:34.464 --> 00:01:35.678
Step three, Check.

37
00:01:35.678 --> 00:01:37.247
A screening at touchdown:

38
00:01:37.247 --> 00:01:38.916
GO, GO WITH CARE, or NO GO,

39
00:01:38.916 --> 00:01:40.586
with a line each for power,

40
00:01:40.586 --> 00:01:42.104
Earth contact and slope.

41
00:01:42.104 --> 00:01:42.660
Here,

42
00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:45.342
Earth clears the skyline by less than a degree,

43
00:01:45.342 --> 00:01:46.455
so it warns you:

44
00:01:46.455 --> 00:01:47.669
close to the line.

45
00:01:48.319 --> 00:01:50.580
Every result names the terrain it used.

46
00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:53.996
How this was worked out lists the method and every assumption.

47
00:01:53.996 --> 00:01:56.106
It's a screening with your settings,

48
00:01:56.106 --> 00:01:57.663
never a landing approval.

49
00:01:58.313 --> 00:01:59.610
Step four, What if.

50
00:01:59.610 --> 00:02:02.100
Move the site a kilometre, shift the date,

51
00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:04.072
or check the whole landing area,

52
00:02:04.072 --> 00:02:06.718
and it tells you in plain words what changes.

53
00:02:07.368 --> 00:02:09.444
Flight shows how you'd get there:

54
00:02:09.444 --> 00:02:12.159
launch date, route, every burn, and the fuel.

55
00:02:12.159 --> 00:02:14.448
Compare a direct trip, phasing loops,

56
00:02:14.448 --> 00:02:16.205
and a slow low-energy path.

57
00:02:16.205 --> 00:02:19.080
Each burn says whether it's computed or assumed.

58
00:02:19.730 --> 00:02:21.730
Compare puts plans side by side:

59
00:02:21.730 --> 00:02:23.361
other sites, other dates,

60
00:02:23.361 --> 00:02:25.518
or your plan against the best spot,

61
00:02:25.518 --> 00:02:27.623
with every measure drawn to scale.

62
00:02:28.273 --> 00:02:29.643
Step five, Report.

63
00:02:29.643 --> 00:02:32.097
It opens as a column beside the Moon.

64
00:02:32.097 --> 00:02:34.950
Expand it to read everything, then print it,

65
00:02:34.950 --> 00:02:37.233
download the data, or copy a link.

66
00:02:37.883 --> 00:02:39.148
For a closer look,

67
00:02:39.148 --> 00:02:42.786
Terrain 3D puts you on NASA's twenty-metre terrain at the site,

68
00:02:42.786 --> 00:02:44.789
with the Sun and Earth drawn in.

69
00:02:44.789 --> 00:02:47.056
Back takes you straight to your plan.

70
00:02:47.706 --> 00:02:48.344
Stuck?

71
00:02:48.344 --> 00:02:51.106
Every view has its own guided tour under Help.

72
00:02:51.106 --> 00:02:52.807
Ask Lumi in plain English,

73
00:02:52.807 --> 00:02:56.154
and the accuracy page shows how every number was checked.

74
00:02:56.804 --> 00:03:01.257
Positions match NASA JPL Horizons to within a hundredth of a degree,

75
00:03:01.257 --> 00:03:06.914
and shadows match Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter photos on ninety-three percent of pixels.

76
00:03:06.914 --> 00:03:09.381
Free, open source, in your browser.

77
00:03:09.381 --> 00:03:10.585
Happy landing.
